From b363428cfc7c4670b9da52ad79dce8d5390083f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domingo Alvarez Duarte <mingo@Domingos-MacBook-Air.local>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 13:21:50 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 5/8] feat(generate): add `--lalr` and `--max-states`, and
 report progress

Canonical LR(1) states are what make `generate` infeasible for grammars
that are written for yacc/bison: their state count grows with the number of
distinct lookahead sets per kernel item, and for a grammar of a real
language it explodes (the PostgreSQL 16 grammar needs 354k states, and
`generate` never finishes). Bison users have always been fine with LALR(1),
which merges states that have the same kernel items and unions their
lookaheads.

Add `OptLevel::Lalr` and the `--lalr` flag: when a new state has the same
kernel core (`ParseItemSetCore`) as an existing state, merge the lookahead
sets into the existing state and re-run it, resetting the actions that its
lookaheads produced. Lookahead sets only grow, so this reaches a fixpoint.
`ParseStateInfo::item_sets_by_ids` becomes a plain `Vec` indexed by state
id, since LALR lookup is by id rather than by full item set.

Keep it opt-in: it can report conflicts that canonical LR(1) resolves away,
so it should not silently change what existing grammars accept.

Also add `--max-states N` to turn a state explosion into an error instead
of a process to kill, and a stage-timing line every 2s under
`TS_DEBUG_TIMING` so a slow stage is visible while it runs.

PostgreSQL 16 (1195 rules): >90s/killed, 942MB -> 4.1s, 87MB.
